# React Component Conventions

This document defines the coding standards for React components in this project.

## File References
@components/JsonInput.tsx
@components/TreeViewer.tsx
@lib/types.ts

## File Structure

Every component file should follow this structure:

```tsx
/**
 * Component Name
 * 
 * Brief description of what this component does.
 * Key features and responsibilities.
 */

'use client';  // If component uses client-side features

import { useState, useRef } from 'react';  // React imports first
import { SomeType } from '@/lib/types';     // Local type imports
import { someUtil } from '@/lib/someUtils'; // Local utility imports
import ChildComponent from './ChildComponent'; // Relative component imports

interface ComponentNameProps {
    // Props definition
}

export default function ComponentName({ prop1, prop2 }: ComponentNameProps) {
    // Component implementation
}
```

## Naming Conventions

### Files
- Components: PascalCase (e.g., `JsonInput.tsx`, `TreeViewer.tsx`)
- Utilities: camelCase (e.g., `jsonUtils.ts`, `clipboardUtils.ts`)

### Components
- Use PascalCase: `JsonInput`, `TreeNodeComponent`
- Name should describe what it is, not what it does

### Props Interfaces
- Use `ComponentNameProps` pattern
- Define inline above the component (not in separate types.ts unless shared)

```tsx
interface JsonInputProps {
    value: string;
    onChange: (json: string) => void;
    error: string | null;
}
```

### Event Handlers
- Prefix with `handle`: `handleClick`, `handleTextChange`, `handleDragOver`
- For callbacks passed as props, use `on` prefix: `onChange`, `onNodeClick`

### State Variables
- Use descriptive camelCase: `isDragging`, `selectedNode`, `expandedPaths`

## Component Patterns

### Controlled Components
Components should be controlled by parent state:

```tsx
// Good: Controlled component
export default function JsonInput({ value, onChange }: JsonInputProps) {
    const handleChange = (e: ChangeEvent<HTMLTextAreaElement>) => {
        onChange(e.target.value);  // Delegate to parent
    };
    return <textarea value={value} onChange={handleChange} />;
}

// Bad: Uncontrolled with internal state
export default function JsonInput() {
    const [value, setValue] = useState('');  // Don't manage shared state internally
}
```

### Early Returns for Empty/Error States
Handle edge cases at the top of the component:

```tsx
export default function TreeViewer({ rootNode }: TreeViewerProps) {
    // Early return for empty state
    if (!rootNode) {
        return (
            <div className="...">
                <p>NO DATA DETECTED</p>
            </div>
        );
    }

    // Main render logic
    return (
        <div className="...">
            {/* Tree content */}
        </div>
    );
}
```

### Conditional Rendering
Use logical AND for conditional UI elements:

```tsx
{error && (
    <div className="error-panel">
        <p>{error}</p>
    </div>
)}

{!value && !error && (
    <div className="helper-text">
        <p>WAITING FOR INPUT...</p>
    </div>
)}
```

## TypeScript Practices

### Props Typing
- Always define Props interface
- Use `string | null` instead of optional for nullable values that should be explicitly handled

```tsx
interface JsonInputProps {
    value: string;           // Required
    onChange: (json: string) => void;  // Callback
    error: string | null;    // Explicitly nullable
}
```

### Event Types
Import and use React event types:

```tsx
import { ChangeEvent } from 'react';

const handleChange = (e: ChangeEvent<HTMLTextAreaElement>) => {
    // ...
};

const handleDragOver = (e: React.DragEvent) => {
    e.preventDefault();
};
```

### Generic Types
Use `unknown` instead of `any` for JSON values:

```tsx
// Good
value: unknown;

// Bad
value: any;
```

## Import Order

1. React imports (`import { useState } from 'react'`)
2. External library imports
3. Local type imports (`@/lib/types`)
4. Local utility imports (`@/lib/jsonUtils`)
5. Relative component imports (`./ChildComponent`)

## JSDoc Comments

Add JSDoc at the top of each component file:

```tsx
/**
 * JSON Input Component
 * 
 * Left panel component that handles JSON input via:
 * - Direct paste/typing
 * - File upload
 * - Validation and error display
 * 
 * This component is controlled - it receives JSON and calls onChange
 */
```

## Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS classes
- Reference CSS variables from globals.css for theme support
- Use `.theme-button`, `.theme-panel`, `.digital-text` utility classes

```tsx
<div className="bg-[var(--bg-panel)] border border-[var(--border-main)]">
    <button className="theme-button px-2 py-1">CLICK</button>
</div>
```
